Produce a price-direction forecast for {{ symbol }} (NSE) as of {{ as_of }}.

1. Call the market data tool: fetch_ohlcv(symbol="{{ symbol }}", market="NSE", start="{{ start_date }}", end="{{ as_of }}", as_of="{{ as_of }}").
   It returns {symbol, market, bars: [{date, open, high, low, close, volume}, ...], data_stale}, oldest bar first.
   Immediately use write_file to save that exact tool result to /workspace/bars.json. Do this
   once, right after fetching -- do NOT retype, re-paste, or hand-copy any dates/prices from the
   tool output into a script anywhere below; always load them back with
   `json.load(open('/workspace/bars.json'))`.

2. In Python (under {{ workspace_path }}), compute quantitative features and train an ML/econometric forecasting model:
   - Always load data dynamically with `json.load(open('/workspace/bars.json'))`.
   - Engineer feature signals: lagged log returns (1d, 3d, 5d, 10d), rolling realized volatility, RSI/MFI momentum, volume ratios, and Kalman-filtered trend (`filterpy`, `scipy`).
   - Fit predictive models using installed ML/econometric libraries (`lightgbm`, `catboost`, `scikit-learn`, `statsmodels` AutoReg/ARIMA, `arch` GARCH).
   - Fit and predict walk-forward only: for each step `i`, fit using only bars strictly before `i`
     (an expanding or rolling window ending at `i-1`), then predict step `i`. Never fit once on
     the full series and evaluate on the same data -- that leaks future information into
     `forecasts[i]`/`calls[i]` and makes the M8 backtest meaningless even though it will still run.
   - Generate out-of-sample predicted return `forecasts[i]` and advance probability `calls[i] = P(return > 0)`.
   - Set `trade_side[i] = "buy"` if `calls[i] > 0.5`, else `"hold"`.
   - Set `position_notional[i] = 10000.0` when `trade_side[i] == "buy"`, else `0.0`.

3. {{ macros.eval_contract(symbol, as_of) }}

4. Separately, as your structured final response, give your own overall forecast for {{ symbol }}
   over the next {{ horizon_days }} day by calling the AgentSignal tool:
   - `agent_name`: "price"
   - `direction`: "up" or "down"
   - `probability`: 0.50 to 1.0 (calibrated probability of your chosen direction occurring; e.g. 0.65 for 65% chance of UP if direction="up", or 0.65 for 65% chance of DOWN if direction="down")
   - `confidence`: 0.0 to 1.0
   - `horizon_days`: 1
   - `evidence`: list of observations citing `source_capability: "market_data"` or `"macro"`. Every
     `value` must be a number or fact you actually read from the saved JSON files -- never
     estimate, round from memory, or invent a plausible-looking number.

IMPORTANT: You MUST conclude your turn by calling the AgentSignal structured response tool.
